      "description": "FLAGSHIP SIMULATION — no initial position is implied.\n\nContext: UNESCO’s Open Science Recommendation frames openness as access to knowledge, infrastructure, societal engagement and dialogue with diverse knowledge systems, while emphasizing quality, equity and capacity.\n\nTrigger: A fictional consortium must design a common open-science policy for publicly funded research across institutions with very different infrastructure and funding capacity.\n\nDecision task: Agree on minimum openness rules, exceptions, infrastructure responsibilities, incentives, capacity measures and monitoring.\n\nCommon evidence baseline: Recommendation on Open Science; Implementation of the UNESCO Recommendation on Open Science; First consultation on implementation of the 2021 Recommendation on Open Science; Biodiversity: Local, indigenous and scientific knowledge.\n\nStructured casting, evidence URLs, uncertainties, event injects, round plan and cross-World links are in the seed flagship source file.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "open-science"
    },
    {
      "key": "biodiversity_evidence_thresholds",
      "title": "[DEMO] What evidence threshold should guide action when biodiversity risks are uncertain?",
      "description": "FLAGSHIP SIMULATION — no initial position is implied.\n\nContext: UNESCO notes persistent biodiversity data gaps, unequal science capacity and the importance of combining scientific, Indigenous and local knowledge without collapsing their different methods and governance conditions.\n\nTrigger: A fictional regional authority must decide whether to restrict a high-impact activity while monitoring data are incomplete and local knowledge indicates ecosystem change.\n\nDecision task: Define an evidence threshold, precaution conditions, monitoring plan, knowledge-governance safeguards and criteria for revising the decision.\n\nCommon evidence baseline: Biodiversity: Local, indigenous and scientific knowledge; National Outlook on Indigenous and Local Knowledge of Biodiversity; Advancing Indigenous and local knowledge for biodiversity policy and action; Recommendation on Open Science.\n\nStructured casting, evidence URLs, uncertainties, event injects, round plan and cross-World links are in the seed flagship source file.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "environment"
    },
    {
      "key": "climate_adaptation_priorities",
      "title": "[DEMO] How should limited climate-adaptation resources be prioritized across communities and infrastructure?",
      "description": "Deliberate scientific evidence, planning, distributional effects, economics and legal responsibilities.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "science-policy"
    },
    {
      "key": "scientific_data_governance",
      "title": "[DEMO] Which governance model best supports international sharing of high-value scientific data?",
      "description": "Examine interoperability, stewardship, access, legal constraints and statistical reliability.",
      "status": "open",
      "category": "science-data"
    }
  ],
